# Omnius — secrets & machine config
# Copy this file to .env and fill in the values. NEVER commit .env.
# Claude sessions are denied reading .env (see .claude\settings.json); scripts/tools consume it.

# --- Discord bot (Phase 2) ---
# Create the app + bot at https://discord.com/developers/applications
# and invite it to your private server. It needs eight scoped permissions -
# Manage Channels, View Channels, Send Messages, Read Message History,
# Embed Links, Attach Files, Add Reactions, Manage Messages (permissions=126032)
# - not admin (GETTING-STARTED.md walks through it; docs\DISCORD.md par.4 says
# what each one is for).
DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=
DISCORD_GUILD_ID=
# Your Discord user ID (Settings → Advanced → Developer Mode on, then right-click your name → Copy User ID).
# The watchdog obeys ONLY this user — messages from anyone/anything else are ignored.
DISCORD_OWNER_ID=

# --- Everything below moved to config\ on 2026-08-05 ---
# MACHINE_NAME, HEARTBEAT_MINUTES and DISCORD_GATEWAY now live in
# config\omnius.ini, which is commented and explains each one. They are still
# read from here if you set them - environment beats config file beats default -
# so nothing breaks if you leave them. New installs should use config\.
# See config\README.md.

# --- This machine ---
# Optional label shown in session claims and Discord channel topics (e.g. main-pc, laptop).
# Leave empty to auto-detect the computer name.
MACHINE_NAME=

# --- Heartbeat ---
# Minutes between heartbeat CHECKS while the watchdog runs. 0 = off.
# The check is cheap (a few file stats and a clock read) and only wakes Omnius
# when it finds something mechanical to do - stale claims, the daily briefing
# after 07:00, Monday's memory gardening. In practice that is a couple of wakes
# a day, not one every 30 minutes. See memory\orchestrator\HEARTBEAT.md.
HEARTBEAT_MINUTES=30

# --- Gateway ---
# 0 forces plain REST polling instead of the Discord Gateway websocket.
# Leave unset unless the socket is misbehaving; REST is ~3s slower per message.
DISCORD_GATEWAY=1

# --- Mail passwords (one per account in config\email.ini) ---
# The account itself - host, port, address - is described in config\email.ini.
# ONLY the password lives here, and config names this key rather than holding
# the value (config\README.md, rule 2). The key name is yours: whatever you
# write as `password_env` in that account's block is what is read here.
#
# Use an APP PASSWORD for Zoho, Gmail and Outlook. Those accounts have 2FA and
# will reject your normal login password with an error that looks like a
# network fault.
#
# Quote the value if it contains " #" - an unquoted inline comment marker
# truncates it. Internal spaces are fine and are preserved.
# EMAIL_WORK_PASSWORD=
# EMAIL_PERSONAL_PASSWORD=

# --- Website passwords (one per site in config\websites.ini) ---
# The site itself - url, user, selectors - is described in config\websites.ini.
# ONLY the password lives here, and config names this key rather than holding
# the value (config\README.md, rule 2). The key name is yours: whatever you
# write as `password_env` in that site's block is what is read here.
#
# Desks are DENIED from reading this file, so a password never enters a
# session's context: tools\playwright\weblogin.py resolves it, types it into
# the browser and saves the session. See docs\WEB.md.
#
# Safest of all is no entry at all: sign in by hand once in your own browser
# and let desks drive the logged-in page with the Chrome extension.
# WEB_IONOS_PWD=

# --- Mistral: reading documents (RECOMMENDED - this one is close to free) ---
# Not in the optional block below on purpose. PDFs that already contain text are
# read locally with no key at all, but a SCANNED page or a photographed invoice
# is just a picture until something does OCR on it - and that is most of the
# paperwork anyone actually needs read. It also does structured extraction, so an
# invoice comes back as fields rather than as a wall of text.
#   https://console.mistral.ai  ->  API Keys
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=

# --- AI capabilities (image, video, speech) ---
# WHICH capability uses WHICH key is chosen in config\ai.ini; this file only
# holds the values. Every one of these is optional: with none of them set,
# Omnius runs exactly as it does today and each capability simply stays off.
#
# ONE Google key covers images, video AND text-to-speech - one provider, one
# secret, one invoice. That is the whole reason to prefer it.
#   https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# Only needed for OpenAI image models (gpt-image-2).
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Only needed if you pick elevenlabs for speech. Google is cheaper and the
# built-in Windows voices cost nothing at all.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# --- Telegram: inviting one person who has no Discord account ---
# Optional, and off until config\telegram.ini exists. ONE bot serves everyone you
# invite: each person writes to it and reaches exactly ONE of your channels.
# Create the bot with @BotFather on Telegram, paste the token it gives you:
#   https://t.me/botfather  ->  /newbot
# The token is a bearer credential - it lives here, never in config\ and never
# in a memory file. See tools\telegram\README.md.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
